WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.549 --> 00:00:03.049
(light music)

2
00:00:02.000 --> 00:00:07.000
Downloaded from
YTS.MX

3
00:00:07.071 --> 00:00:09.230
- [Narrator] You have the
feeling you're by yourself

4
00:00:08.000 --> 00:00:13.000
Official YIFY movies site:
YTS.MX

5
00:00:09.230 --> 00:00:12.070
in the forest and you
have the strange sensation

6
00:00:12.070 --> 00:00:13.620
you're not alone.

7
00:00:13.620 --> 00:00:18.133
Your heart starts pounding
and then, a squirrel.

8
00:00:19.200 --> 00:00:22.250
The forest is the original
home of this little creature

9
00:00:22.250 --> 00:00:25.113
who makes every walk through
the woods an adventure.

10
00:00:27.410 --> 00:00:31.100
Lively, nimble-footed
squirrels enchant young

11
00:00:31.100 --> 00:00:32.273
and old alike.

12
00:00:35.900 --> 00:00:38.300
But what makes them different?

13
00:00:38.300 --> 00:00:39.883
What's their secret recipe?

14
00:00:41.970 --> 00:00:44.950
Let yourself be enchanted
by the secret life

15
00:00:44.950 --> 00:00:47.913
of squirrels and their
fascinating characters.

16
00:00:50.394 --> 00:00:52.894
(light music)

17
00:00:57.550 --> 00:01:00.710
In a mixed forest somewhere
in central Europe,

18
00:01:00.710 --> 00:01:04.503
far above our heads something
magical is taking place.

19
00:01:08.680 --> 00:01:11.180
A squirrel is becoming a mother.

20
00:01:11.180 --> 00:01:13.980
Like she does every spring,
the female has built

21
00:01:13.980 --> 00:01:17.180
her nest in a protected tree
trunk and has cushioned it

22
00:01:17.180 --> 00:01:18.013
with moss.

23
00:01:23.690 --> 00:01:26.430
The baby is born feet first.

24
00:01:26.430 --> 00:01:28.050
It's her third.

25
00:01:28.050 --> 00:01:29.250
Another one will follow.

26
00:01:39.370 --> 00:01:42.353
A female can have up to
six young in one litter.

27
00:01:48.740 --> 00:01:52.273
The big wide world awaits
four baby squirrels.

28
00:01:54.330 --> 00:01:57.850
And their world looks something like this,

29
00:01:57.850 --> 00:01:59.753
at least from a squirrel's eye view.

30
00:02:01.060 --> 00:02:04.530
Squirrels have conquered
almost the entire earth.

31
00:02:04.530 --> 00:02:08.990
Maybe 280 different species
live on four continents.

32
00:02:08.990 --> 00:02:12.630
Only Australia and
Antarctica are squirrel free.

33
00:02:12.630 --> 00:02:15.570
The differences between
them are as far apart

34
00:02:15.570 --> 00:02:17.330
as their ranges.

35
00:02:17.330 --> 00:02:19.830
(light music)

36
00:02:28.970 --> 00:02:31.290
Fall in North America.

37
00:02:31.290 --> 00:02:33.910
It's not only leaves
that fall from the trees

38
00:02:37.740 --> 00:02:39.143
but also acorns.

39
00:02:41.270 --> 00:02:44.233
It's harvest time and it's raining food.

40
00:02:45.410 --> 00:02:48.803
This is a job for chipmunks.

41
00:02:49.760 --> 00:02:52.193
This is just what
they've been waiting for.

42
00:02:53.070 --> 00:02:55.410
Chipmunks are probably
the best known relatives

43
00:02:55.410 --> 00:02:58.080
of our native European
squirrel and the sound

44
00:02:58.080 --> 00:03:01.323
of falling acorns brings them up to speed.

45
00:03:05.460 --> 00:03:08.340
Now begins the time of abundance.

46
00:03:08.340 --> 00:03:11.350
There's food all over
the forest just waiting

47
00:03:11.350 --> 00:03:12.453
to be collected.

48
00:03:13.730 --> 00:03:16.200
But there's no time to hesitate since snow

49
00:03:16.200 --> 00:03:17.743
will soon cover everything.

50
00:03:18.880 --> 00:03:21.350
Chipmunks can't build up fat reserves

51
00:03:21.350 --> 00:03:24.470
so they have to squirrel
away as many nuts as possible

52
00:03:24.470 --> 00:03:25.723
for their hibernation.

53
00:03:30.300 --> 00:03:33.610
Every acorn is tested
for its preservability

54
00:03:33.610 --> 00:03:37.373
and makes it way into the
big elastic cheek pouches.

55
00:03:38.220 --> 00:03:40.873
There's room in there
for up to seven nuts.

56
00:03:41.750 --> 00:03:46.540
This saves running around and
with so much energy at that.

57
00:04:00.440 --> 00:04:02.430
Countless trips are needed to fill up

58
00:04:02.430 --> 00:04:04.153
such an underground cache.

59
00:04:06.151 --> 00:04:08.651
(light music)

60
00:04:25.640 --> 00:04:29.593
The chipmunks hustle and bustle
don't go unnoticed for long.

61
00:04:30.530 --> 00:04:34.480
His neighbor collects leaves
for a cozy sleeping chamber.

62
00:04:34.480 --> 00:04:37.103
That takes time away from nut gathering.

63
00:04:38.550 --> 00:04:40.923
But why go long distances?

64
00:04:43.960 --> 00:04:44.910
The coast is clear.

65
00:04:45.966 --> 00:04:48.966
(suspenseful music)

66
00:04:53.380 --> 00:04:58.380
Leaves in, nuts out.

67
00:05:00.770 --> 00:05:02.163
A good exchange.

68
00:05:03.120 --> 00:05:06.713
Open doors make thieves of squirrels too.

69
00:05:08.720 --> 00:05:11.023
But woe to those who are caught.

70
00:05:17.400 --> 00:05:20.530
The underground nut
stores are life insurance

71
00:05:20.530 --> 00:05:22.090
for chipmunks.

72
00:05:22.090 --> 00:05:25.313
Without them they couldn't
survive the harsh winter.

73
00:05:30.050 --> 00:05:34.718
That's why the fun is over
when it comes to nuts.

74
00:05:34.718 --> 00:05:37.718
(suspenseful music)

75
00:05:48.170 --> 00:05:51.120
Chipmunks are solitary creatures.

76
00:05:51.120 --> 00:05:53.740
Normally they'd ignore one another.

77
00:05:53.740 --> 00:05:56.540
For other kinds of squirrels, cooperation

78
00:05:56.540 --> 00:05:58.423
with others is essential to survival.

79
00:06:00.566 --> 00:06:03.066
(light music)

80
00:06:05.750 --> 00:06:08.410
On the treeless North American prairie,

81
00:06:08.410 --> 00:06:10.890
little mounds of earth are within site

82
00:06:10.890 --> 00:06:12.650
and hearing of one another.

83
00:06:19.720 --> 00:06:24.010
Prairie dogs are communication experts.

84
00:06:24.010 --> 00:06:27.030
Their calls roll across the endless plain

85
00:06:27.030 --> 00:06:29.473
like an acoustic Mexican wave.

86
00:06:31.500 --> 00:06:34.490
The guards confirm their
vigilance with each other

87
00:06:34.490 --> 00:06:37.160
so individuals in the colony can go about

88
00:06:37.160 --> 00:06:39.183
their favorite business undeterred.

89
00:06:45.460 --> 00:06:47.823
For instance, digging.

90
00:06:52.330 --> 00:06:55.333
Or just sparring with
a member of the family.

91
00:07:06.410 --> 00:07:09.430
Despite their name,
prairie dogs are not dogs

92
00:07:09.430 --> 00:07:12.640
but get their names from
their dog-like barking.

93
00:07:12.640 --> 00:07:15.320
Yet this isn't just meaningless yelping.

94
00:07:15.320 --> 00:07:17.060
It's a language.

95
00:07:17.060 --> 00:07:19.870
Prairie dogs have developed
an individual warning call

96
00:07:19.870 --> 00:07:23.083
for each enemy, which
everybody understands.

97
00:07:33.550 --> 00:07:36.710
They have to keep the
grass short so they can see

98
00:07:36.710 --> 00:07:38.650
potential intruders.

99
00:07:38.650 --> 00:07:41.483
It's the only way they
can keep an overview.

100
00:07:50.740 --> 00:07:53.570
Prairie dogs are rarely alone.

101
00:07:53.570 --> 00:07:56.320
Though the grassland
looks deserted at first,

102
00:07:56.320 --> 00:07:59.433
unannounced visitors could always come by.

103
00:08:03.930 --> 00:08:06.900
That's why every prairie dweller is kept

104
00:08:06.900 --> 00:08:08.103
careful track of.

105
00:08:14.758 --> 00:08:16.927
(squeaking)

106
00:08:16.927 --> 00:08:19.940
If the squirrel guard spies a robber,

107
00:08:19.940 --> 00:08:22.183
he gives off an individual warning call.

108
00:08:23.546 --> 00:08:26.410
(squeaking)

109
00:08:26.410 --> 00:08:29.200
And automatically sets off the appropriate

110
00:08:29.200 --> 00:08:31.343
flight measures within the colony.

111
00:08:33.512 --> 00:08:36.050
The whistle sounds
monotonous to the human ear.

112
00:08:36.050 --> 00:08:38.683
Yet to the prairie dog it's unmistakable.

113
00:08:43.530 --> 00:08:46.780
The sounds are so specific
that the ground squirrels

114
00:08:46.780 --> 00:08:49.710
know exactly that they're
dealing with a coyote,

115
00:08:49.710 --> 00:08:53.373
how far away it is, and which
direction it's coming from.

116
00:08:55.109 --> 00:08:58.109
(suspenseful music)

117
00:09:13.540 --> 00:09:17.540
A danger foreseen is a danger avoided.

118
00:09:17.540 --> 00:09:20.861
The better prairie dogs
communicate with each other,

119
00:09:20.861 --> 00:09:22.711
the better their chance for survival.

120
00:09:23.665 --> 00:09:25.998
(squeaking)

121
00:09:42.550 --> 00:09:45.140
A journey to the other
side of the earth takes us

122
00:09:45.140 --> 00:09:47.604
to a very different world.

123
00:09:47.604 --> 00:09:50.104
(light music)

124
00:09:58.400 --> 00:10:03.070
Extensive tea plantations and
thick evergreen woodlands.

125
00:10:03.070 --> 00:10:04.643
We're in southwest India.

126
00:10:05.928 --> 00:10:08.428
(light music)

127
00:10:20.660 --> 00:10:24.290
An area that despite all
the people still shelters

128
00:10:24.290 --> 00:10:28.690
unique wildlife, like
the lion-tailed macaque,

129
00:10:28.690 --> 00:10:31.163
one of the rarest primates in the world.

130
00:10:32.395 --> 00:10:35.312
(monkey squawking)

131
00:10:39.520 --> 00:10:43.093
A very special relative of
our squirrel lives here,

132
00:10:45.060 --> 00:10:47.303
the Indian giant squirrel.

133
00:10:49.610 --> 00:10:53.073
Big as a monkey, it's a
Goliath among squirrels.

134
00:11:04.230 --> 00:11:07.240
They live only in the treetops
of the Indian rainforest

135
00:11:07.240 --> 00:11:09.523
and almost never need
to frequent the ground.

136
00:11:10.388 --> 00:11:12.153
Their walkways are the canopies.

137
00:11:16.606 --> 00:11:19.523
(monkey squawking)

138
00:11:34.990 --> 00:11:37.810
Lion-tailed macaque's only a bit bigger

139
00:11:37.810 --> 00:11:40.633
than the giant squirrels,
share the canopies with them.

140
00:11:41.570 --> 00:11:44.350
But encounters between the two are not

141
00:11:44.350 --> 00:11:45.703
necessarily desirable.

142
00:11:49.170 --> 00:11:52.573
A narrow squirrel road
20 meters off the ground.

143
00:11:53.480 --> 00:11:55.863
The squirrel doesn't give way voluntarily.

144
00:11:57.640 --> 00:11:59.863
They each have only one goal.

145
00:12:06.350 --> 00:12:08.353
Giant overripe jackfruit.

146
00:12:10.570 --> 00:12:13.640
These heavy fruits
weighing up to 30 kilograms

147
00:12:13.640 --> 00:12:17.400
hang on tree trunks shortly
before the rainy season.

148
00:12:17.400 --> 00:12:19.993
The forest is permeated by their smell.

149
00:12:21.180 --> 00:12:25.300
The giant squirrel effortlessly
gnaws the hard shell open

150
00:12:25.300 --> 00:12:27.603
to get to the nutritious sticky fruit.

151
00:12:31.090 --> 00:12:34.380
For a short period of time,
jackfruit are the main source

152
00:12:34.380 --> 00:12:36.203
of food for many forest dwellers.

153
00:12:38.023 --> 00:12:41.290
(light music)

154
00:12:41.290 --> 00:12:43.603
The best fruits are the hardest to reach.

155
00:12:45.010 --> 00:12:47.510
(light music)

156
00:13:05.640 --> 00:13:10.040
How nice would it be to have
a fruit that's already open

157
00:13:10.040 --> 00:13:11.507
like this one.

158
00:13:12.810 --> 00:13:15.810
(suspenseful music)

159
00:13:28.080 --> 00:13:28.913
Much better.

160
00:13:33.800 --> 00:13:37.413
But the giant squirrel is
not considering retreat.

161
00:13:38.340 --> 00:13:41.330
The Indian forest in June is full of fruit

162
00:13:41.330 --> 00:13:44.030
and yet they both got their eye on just

163
00:13:44.030 --> 00:13:45.910
this one specimen.

164
00:13:45.910 --> 00:13:49.213
Partly peeled ones are
particularly highly prized.

165
00:13:50.176 --> 00:13:53.670
Neither wants to clear the field.

166
00:13:53.670 --> 00:13:56.670
(suspenseful music)

167
00:14:00.970 --> 00:14:05.200
Following some back and forth,
the monkey finally gives up.

168
00:14:05.200 --> 00:14:07.043
No pain, no gain.

169
00:14:07.920 --> 00:14:10.913
He'll have to crack
his next fruit himself.

170
00:14:13.950 --> 00:14:16.920
The giant squirrel's
tenacity has paid off,

171
00:14:16.920 --> 00:14:18.770
another character trait that has helped

172
00:14:18.770 --> 00:14:21.720
all his cousins on their
triumphal march through the world.

173
00:14:26.180 --> 00:14:29.323
Things are peaceful in our
Eurasian red squirrel's tree.

174
00:14:30.350 --> 00:14:33.840
The young have grown quickly
and at nearly six weeks,

175
00:14:33.840 --> 00:14:36.353
they already look like real squirrels.

176
00:14:38.730 --> 00:14:41.573
It's starting to get crowded in there.

177
00:14:44.825 --> 00:14:47.408
(upbeat music)

178
00:14:52.660 --> 00:14:55.750
Scuffles among the
siblings can't be avoided

179
00:14:55.750 --> 00:14:57.123
in such a small place.

180
00:14:58.339 --> 00:15:00.922
(upbeat music)

181
00:15:15.640 --> 00:15:18.560
The young squirrels
playfully test their agility

182
00:15:18.560 --> 00:15:21.660
and jumping power, improve
their physical condition

183
00:15:21.660 --> 00:15:23.723
and develop their motor skills.

184
00:15:26.600 --> 00:15:29.070
Soon they'll leave the nest on their own

185
00:15:29.070 --> 00:15:32.743
and will have to prove their
prowess as tree acrobats.

186
00:15:35.250 --> 00:15:37.750
(light music)

187
00:15:52.780 --> 00:15:55.873
Mid winter in the far north of America.

188
00:16:04.750 --> 00:16:08.570
Freezing cold and darkness are too extreme

189
00:16:08.570 --> 00:16:09.813
for many animals.

190
00:16:10.740 --> 00:16:13.273
And so they migrate south.

191
00:16:15.286 --> 00:16:18.119
(whimsical music)

192
00:16:24.410 --> 00:16:28.191
Yet there's secret life in
this northern winter night.

193
00:16:28.191 --> 00:16:31.024
(whimsical music)

194
00:16:35.300 --> 00:16:38.380
As one of the few nocturnal
squirrels, these little

195
00:16:38.380 --> 00:16:40.810
tree dwellers must stay
permanently in motion

196
00:16:40.810 --> 00:16:43.190
so that they don't freeze.

197
00:16:43.190 --> 00:16:46.772
Lichens, tree fungi and small
seeds are their only sources

198
00:16:46.772 --> 00:16:48.883
of energy at these latitudes.

199
00:17:04.379 --> 00:17:06.879
(owl hooting)

200
00:17:08.500 --> 00:17:12.900
Despite this extreme cold,
these animals don't hibernate.

201
00:17:12.900 --> 00:17:15.980
They stay awake scurrying
like miniature goblins

202
00:17:15.980 --> 00:17:18.503
up and down the tree trunks
throughout the night.

203
00:17:26.290 --> 00:17:30.270
The trees that provide them
with food are mostly set apart

204
00:17:30.270 --> 00:17:33.040
and the path across the
snowy ground consumes

205
00:17:33.040 --> 00:17:34.910
far too much energy.

206
00:17:34.910 --> 00:17:36.893
And it's dangerous.

207
00:17:37.810 --> 00:17:40.150
But how to reach a goal
that's outside squirrel

208
00:17:40.150 --> 00:17:43.623
jumping distance, with a special trick.

209
00:17:46.432 --> 00:17:50.810
A powerful jump and a
flying squirrel sails

210
00:17:50.810 --> 00:17:51.710
through the night.

211
00:17:55.910 --> 00:17:59.603
A tiny flying carpet in the wintry forest.

212
00:18:12.820 --> 00:18:16.250
At nearly 30 kilometers
per hour, flying squirrels

213
00:18:16.250 --> 00:18:18.830
glide up to 80 meters through the night

214
00:18:18.830 --> 00:18:22.123
aiming directly for food
sources that are far apart.

215
00:18:23.190 --> 00:18:25.773
(owls hooting)

216
00:18:28.243 --> 00:18:30.826
(upbeat music)

217
00:18:50.830 --> 00:18:54.600
This special ability allows
them to cover a lot of ground

218
00:18:54.600 --> 00:18:56.950
with a minimum of effort.

219
00:18:56.950 --> 00:18:59.940
And they're one of the few
mammals to have conquered

220
00:18:59.940 --> 00:19:01.402
air space.

221
00:19:01.402 --> 00:19:03.985
(upbeat music)

222
00:19:20.920 --> 00:19:24.863
A fascinating evolutionary
leap for our squirrel family.

223
00:19:30.840 --> 00:19:34.460
Which wouldn't work at all
in the desert of Arizona.

224
00:19:34.460 --> 00:19:37.830
No flying squirrel would
survive even one landing

225
00:19:37.830 --> 00:19:39.133
on forms like these.

226
00:19:40.511 --> 00:19:43.011
(light music)

227
00:19:50.350 --> 00:19:53.270
During the day, temperatures
in the Sonoran Desert

228
00:19:53.270 --> 00:19:56.620
can reach over 45 degrees Celsius.

229
00:19:56.620 --> 00:19:59.990
The air burns and most
desert dwellers spend the day

230
00:19:59.990 --> 00:20:00.823
in the shade.

231
00:20:07.760 --> 00:20:11.913
The coast is clear for
a heat-loving squirrel.

232
00:20:14.430 --> 00:20:17.480
The Harris's antelope
squirrel really gets going

233
00:20:17.480 --> 00:20:19.650
when the going gets hot.

234
00:20:19.650 --> 00:20:23.130
It can withstand temperatures
of over 40 degrees,

235
00:20:23.130 --> 00:20:26.490
actually life-threatening
for such a little mammal.

236
00:20:26.490 --> 00:20:29.760
But his inner clock tells him
when a heat stroke threatens

237
00:20:29.760 --> 00:20:32.093
and he has to seek shade to cool off.

238
00:20:33.390 --> 00:20:35.900
His cousin, the round
tailed ground squirrel

239
00:20:35.900 --> 00:20:39.110
doesn't leave his shadowy
spot at all at mid day

240
00:20:39.110 --> 00:20:40.103
just to be safe.

241
00:20:46.987 --> 00:20:49.530
Cacti are nutritious but they know how

242
00:20:49.530 --> 00:20:52.710
to protect themselves from hungry mouths.

243
00:20:52.710 --> 00:20:56.790
Their thorny covering keeps
enemies at a distance.

244
00:20:56.790 --> 00:20:58.873
But not the antelope squirrel.

245
00:21:00.490 --> 00:21:03.763
Centimeter long spikes
are no hindrance to them.

246
00:21:04.800 --> 00:21:07.345
At least, not a major one.

247
00:21:07.345 --> 00:21:09.845
(light music)

248
00:21:17.690 --> 00:21:21.220
Like a (mumbles), the squirrel
approaches the tangled thorns

249
00:21:21.220 --> 00:21:22.903
to reach the upper chutes.

250
00:21:23.751 --> 00:21:26.251
(light music)

251
00:21:38.420 --> 00:21:41.770
On the ground floor,
things are more tranquil.

252
00:21:41.770 --> 00:21:44.220
The round tailed ground
squirrel only dares

253
00:21:44.220 --> 00:21:46.660
to go out briefly into the harsh sunlight

254
00:21:46.660 --> 00:21:48.333
then cools off in the shade.

255
00:21:49.220 --> 00:21:51.890
As it does, its body
temperature quickly falls

256
00:21:51.890 --> 00:21:53.313
to a bearable level.

257
00:21:55.770 --> 00:21:59.030
It's even harder to get
past the thorns unscathed

258
00:21:59.030 --> 00:22:00.363
when you're upside down.

259
00:22:01.220 --> 00:22:04.900
Antelope squirrels are
very skillful climbers.

260
00:22:04.900 --> 00:22:07.797
But how they survive running
the gauntlet like this

261
00:22:07.797 --> 00:22:10.653
without being injured
remains their secret.

262
00:22:12.870 --> 00:22:16.433
The squirrel hurries relentlessly
from cactus to cactus.

263
00:22:18.330 --> 00:22:21.320
Its neighbor in the shade
has a different strategy

264
00:22:21.320 --> 00:22:24.650
and this is, don't jump
the gun and stay cool

265
00:22:24.650 --> 00:22:26.343
relaxing in a sand bath.

266
00:22:29.730 --> 00:22:33.321
But he always keeps his eye
on the (mumbles) squirrel.

267
00:22:33.321 --> 00:22:36.321
(suspenseful music)

268
00:22:49.710 --> 00:22:53.210
The decisive moment
approaches at the spiky peak

269
00:22:53.210 --> 00:22:54.820
of the cactus.

270
00:22:54.820 --> 00:22:58.340
The reward for all the climbing
are the energy-rich seeds

271
00:22:58.340 --> 00:23:00.363
in the chutes of the cholla cactus.

272
00:23:06.350 --> 00:23:09.550
There are only dried
branches down below, unless.

273
00:23:12.883 --> 00:23:15.466
(upbeat music)

274
00:23:26.470 --> 00:23:30.120
Now the shading squirrel
gets his money's worth.

275
00:23:30.120 --> 00:23:32.610
Delicacies like this
would otherwise be out

276
00:23:32.610 --> 00:23:33.573
of his reach.

277
00:23:40.010 --> 00:23:43.250
Two strategies, the same reward.

278
00:23:43.250 --> 00:23:47.913
Squirrels are inventive,
especially when it comes to food.

279
00:23:49.181 --> 00:23:51.681
(light music)

280
00:23:56.120 --> 00:24:00.400
15,000 kilometers from
Arizona lies the South African

281
00:24:00.400 --> 00:24:01.633
Kalahari Desert.

282
00:24:08.540 --> 00:24:11.460
A relatively large amount
of rain falls here.

283
00:24:11.460 --> 00:24:14.923
So calling this a desert is
not intrinsically accurate.

284
00:24:16.576 --> 00:24:19.076
(light music)

285
00:24:25.700 --> 00:24:28.100
Meerkats benefit from the rainfall

286
00:24:28.100 --> 00:24:31.210
which causes the landscape
to turn green regularly

287
00:24:31.210 --> 00:24:33.273
and the ground to swarm with insects.

288
00:24:38.810 --> 00:24:41.410
The Kalahari is a dry savanna.

289
00:24:41.410 --> 00:24:45.510
After every downpour, grass
sprouts from the dry ground.

290
00:24:45.510 --> 00:24:47.690
But the chutes have no
chance to grow longer

291
00:24:47.690 --> 00:24:49.750
than 10 centimeters here.

292
00:24:49.750 --> 00:24:53.080
An entire armada of
large and small grazers

293
00:24:53.080 --> 00:24:54.745
make sure of that.

294
00:24:54.745 --> 00:24:57.245
(light music)

295
00:25:05.030 --> 00:25:08.110
Cape ground squirrels
also feast immediately

296
00:25:08.110 --> 00:25:09.363
on the green chutes.

297
00:25:10.700 --> 00:25:13.550
It's not long before the South African sun

298
00:25:13.550 --> 00:25:15.433
burns mercilessly down again.

299
00:25:17.730 --> 00:25:20.483
It's good to have that built-in sunshade.

300
00:25:23.940 --> 00:25:26.600
Cape ground squirrels
are born with a standard

301
00:25:26.600 --> 00:25:28.260
sun protection.

302
00:25:28.260 --> 00:25:30.530
When they look for food
in the blazing sun,

303
00:25:30.530 --> 00:25:32.730
they hold their bushy
tails over their bodies

304
00:25:32.730 --> 00:25:35.883
like umbrellas creating
shade for themselves.

305
00:25:36.750 --> 00:25:38.890
This allows them to keep a cool head

306
00:25:38.890 --> 00:25:40.423
during the heat of the day.

307
00:25:45.670 --> 00:25:47.783
These squirrels live in colonies.

308
00:25:49.455 --> 00:25:51.600
After the rain there's a circus of food

309
00:25:51.600 --> 00:25:53.710
so there's enough time for social contact

310
00:25:53.710 --> 00:25:54.943
and party games.

311
00:25:55.930 --> 00:25:59.050
The colonies visit each
other and play boisterously

312
00:25:59.050 --> 00:26:01.132
across the green savanna.

313
00:26:01.132 --> 00:26:03.715
(upbeat music)

314
00:26:16.969 --> 00:26:19.969
(suspenseful music)

315
00:26:21.300 --> 00:26:24.530
So much activity has lured the cape cobra

316
00:26:24.530 --> 00:26:26.420
from its hiding place.

317
00:26:26.420 --> 00:26:29.010
The snake has its eye
on the young squirrels

318
00:26:29.010 --> 00:26:30.460
in the burrows.

319
00:26:30.460 --> 00:26:32.600
It's bite could be deadly.

320
00:26:32.600 --> 00:26:34.933
But the cape ground squirrel doesn't run.

321
00:26:38.320 --> 00:26:39.770
Quite the opposite.

322
00:26:39.770 --> 00:26:41.843
It gets in the way of the cobra.

323
00:26:44.560 --> 00:26:47.200
Like a protective
shield, it holds its tail

324
00:26:47.200 --> 00:26:49.100
in front of its body.

325
00:26:49.100 --> 00:26:52.223
The squirrel seems bigger
irritating the snake.

326
00:26:56.540 --> 00:26:58.763
The combatants are soon discovered.

327
00:26:59.640 --> 00:27:02.750
All the squirrels in the
colony come to the rescue.

328
00:27:02.750 --> 00:27:06.015
A dance of life and death begins.

329
00:27:06.015 --> 00:27:09.015
(suspenseful music)

330
00:27:15.680 --> 00:27:18.230
The cobra is surrounded.

331
00:27:18.230 --> 00:27:20.013
Its bites are ineffective.

332
00:27:25.940 --> 00:27:29.030
The snake gave up long ago.

333
00:27:29.030 --> 00:27:31.283
It's time for the final attack.

334
00:27:33.480 --> 00:27:35.860
The tide has turned.

335
00:27:35.860 --> 00:27:38.133
The attacker is now the target.

336
00:27:40.683 --> 00:27:43.350
(snake hissing)

337
00:27:46.202 --> 00:27:48.430
And hurries off into the distance.

338
00:27:48.430 --> 00:27:51.003
Not one of its bites has hit its target.

339
00:27:58.620 --> 00:28:02.860
With courage, team spirit
and a multifunctional tool,

340
00:28:02.860 --> 00:28:05.240
cape ground squirrels
withstand the dangers

341
00:28:05.240 --> 00:28:06.323
in the Kalahari.

342
00:28:16.440 --> 00:28:19.300
Their tail also plays
a big role in the lives

343
00:28:19.300 --> 00:28:21.163
of our Eurasian red squirrels.

344
00:28:23.300 --> 00:28:26.170
Like a rudder, it helps
the flying acrobats

345
00:28:26.170 --> 00:28:28.300
to keep their balance.

346
00:28:28.300 --> 00:28:30.830
And when they take
breakneck leaps off trunks

347
00:28:30.830 --> 00:28:34.510
and branches, their tails
maintain their equilibrium.

348
00:28:34.510 --> 00:28:37.190
Their body is perfectly adapted to life

349
00:28:37.190 --> 00:28:38.764
in the treetops.

350
00:28:38.764 --> 00:28:41.347
(upbeat music)

351
00:28:44.340 --> 00:28:47.210
The squirrel is one of the
most accomplished climbers

352
00:28:47.210 --> 00:28:48.558
among mammals.

353
00:28:48.558 --> 00:28:51.058
(light music)

354
00:28:58.285 --> 00:29:01.118
(water splashing)

355
00:29:17.540 --> 00:29:20.990
As perfectly as they
may climb, they dislike

356
00:29:20.990 --> 00:29:22.610
going into water.

357
00:29:22.610 --> 00:29:25.213
Now it could do with some good advice.

358
00:29:27.890 --> 00:29:28.743
It's worth a try.

359
00:29:31.307 --> 00:29:32.140
Careful.

360
00:29:33.448 --> 00:29:35.948
(light music)

361
00:29:39.090 --> 00:29:41.523
The nut is too far from the shore.

362
00:29:46.350 --> 00:29:48.343
The squirrel needs a different tactic.

363
00:29:52.470 --> 00:29:54.133
Maybe from above.

364
00:30:01.120 --> 00:30:02.573
Foiled again.

365
00:30:03.930 --> 00:30:08.163
One more time, raccoon style.

366
00:30:13.200 --> 00:30:16.280
Now using only one hand,
the nut slides back

367
00:30:16.280 --> 00:30:17.603
into the depths again.

368
00:30:22.360 --> 00:30:24.970
Even though sparrow hawks
don't hunt squirrels,

369
00:30:24.970 --> 00:30:27.890
this visit could not be more unwelcome.

370
00:30:27.890 --> 00:30:30.020
The little raptor is in the move to swim

371
00:30:30.020 --> 00:30:31.573
and take things slowly.

372
00:30:32.420 --> 00:30:35.253
First she takes her time
checking the situation.

373
00:30:41.000 --> 00:30:42.480
The squirrel is excited.

374
00:30:43.553 --> 00:30:45.750
But the first thing to do is keep distance

375
00:30:45.750 --> 00:30:46.943
just to make sure.

376
00:31:00.979 --> 00:31:04.250
Onward, the nut has not been forgotten.

377
00:31:04.250 --> 00:31:07.063
The path to the goal has been determined.

378
00:31:10.610 --> 00:31:11.783
Where were we again?

379
00:31:13.120 --> 00:31:16.837
And now two for the price of one.

380
00:31:18.590 --> 00:31:19.853
But one slips.

381
00:31:24.190 --> 00:31:26.393
A last final attempt.

382
00:31:32.610 --> 00:31:35.230
The raccoon method brings success.

383
00:31:35.230 --> 00:31:37.140
This squirrel has learned to get at nuts

384
00:31:37.140 --> 00:31:38.893
that nobody else can find.

385
00:31:48.349 --> 00:31:50.230
On the other end of
the northern hemisphere

386
00:31:50.230 --> 00:31:53.913
a squirrel has also tapped
into an unusual food source.

387
00:31:54.754 --> 00:31:57.254
(light music)

388
00:32:09.870 --> 00:32:12.860
Every summer millions of
salmon swim along the coast

389
00:32:12.860 --> 00:32:14.473
of southwest Alaska.

390
00:32:15.330 --> 00:32:18.860
Fish plays a central role
for all northern wildlife

391
00:32:18.860 --> 00:32:20.483
as it does for grizzlies.

392
00:32:21.530 --> 00:32:25.333
The bears eat almost only
fish for weeks at a time.

393
00:32:27.162 --> 00:32:29.995
(birds squawking)

394
00:32:32.698 --> 00:32:36.531
(bear grunting and splashing)

395
00:32:47.360 --> 00:32:50.670
Much to the delight of the
arctic ground squirrels

396
00:32:50.670 --> 00:32:54.180
which are otherwise
also on the bear's menu.

397
00:32:54.180 --> 00:32:56.650
But when the salmon are
migrating, they have

398
00:32:56.650 --> 00:32:58.470
nothing to fear.

399
00:32:58.470 --> 00:33:01.550
The grizzlies often eat
only the filet pieces

400
00:33:01.550 --> 00:33:02.853
and leave the rest.

401
00:33:12.130 --> 00:33:14.483
Still the squirrels have to be alert.

402
00:33:15.640 --> 00:33:18.090
Summer is the time of
abundance in the far north.

403
00:33:19.100 --> 00:33:21.903
Friend and foe are
constantly out and about.

404
00:33:29.150 --> 00:33:31.343
The fox smells something in the wind.

405
00:33:38.230 --> 00:33:39.800
But he's choosy.

406
00:33:39.800 --> 00:33:42.293
This fish is probably already too old.

407
00:33:45.666 --> 00:33:48.249
(upbeat music)

408
00:33:59.570 --> 00:34:02.773
But the salmon is just
right for the squirrel.

409
00:34:11.680 --> 00:34:14.253
It's a lot of fish for a little squirrel.

410
00:34:20.620 --> 00:34:23.020
But the younger generation
are on their feet

411
00:34:23.020 --> 00:34:24.173
right nearby.

412
00:34:25.039 --> 00:34:27.622
(upbeat music)

413
00:34:31.976 --> 00:34:34.726
And one of them is always hungry.

414
00:34:39.190 --> 00:34:41.930
The young animal has never seen a fish.

415
00:34:41.930 --> 00:34:43.853
He doesn't seem too convinced.

416
00:34:55.800 --> 00:34:58.030
Mother squirrel must first show junior

417
00:34:58.030 --> 00:34:59.323
the right opening.

418
00:35:12.970 --> 00:35:15.610
After a hesitant sampling,
the young squirrel

419
00:35:15.610 --> 00:35:17.460
quickly develops a taste for it

420
00:35:17.460 --> 00:35:20.383
and discovers the grizzly in himself.

421
00:35:30.640 --> 00:35:33.760
The feast is discovered
and the second offspring

422
00:35:33.760 --> 00:35:35.313
doesn't hesitate for long.

423
00:35:36.460 --> 00:35:39.410
Food envy sets in and
the two of them finally

424
00:35:39.410 --> 00:35:42.173
take turns satisfying
their ravenous hunger.

425
00:35:43.196 --> 00:35:45.779
(upbeat music)

426
00:36:04.158 --> 00:36:08.000
Where food is rare, you
can't afford to be picky,

427
00:36:08.000 --> 00:36:10.110
a principle that safeguards the survival

428
00:36:10.110 --> 00:36:13.333
of many squirrel species
in baron landscapes.

429
00:36:14.940 --> 00:36:17.440
(light music)

430
00:36:25.400 --> 00:36:28.080
Several thousand kilometers further south

431
00:36:28.080 --> 00:36:30.370
along the west coast of California,

432
00:36:30.370 --> 00:36:32.460
the hills of the back country are lined

433
00:36:32.460 --> 00:36:33.693
with old oak trees.

434
00:36:39.110 --> 00:36:41.003
There's food here in abundance.

435
00:36:43.330 --> 00:36:44.453
For specialists.

436
00:36:50.300 --> 00:36:53.040
Acorn woodpeckers live
in large family groups

437
00:36:53.040 --> 00:36:54.720
of up to a dozen.

438
00:36:54.720 --> 00:36:57.370
As their name indicates, they specialize

439
00:36:57.370 --> 00:36:58.833
in harvesting acorns.

440
00:37:10.340 --> 00:37:13.380
Each acorn is flow
individually to a central spot

441
00:37:13.380 --> 00:37:14.343
in the territory.

442
00:37:16.140 --> 00:37:17.253
The granary tree.

443
00:37:18.180 --> 00:37:21.070
Winter food supplies are
stored over generations

444
00:37:21.070 --> 00:37:22.638
in such a tree.

445
00:37:22.638 --> 00:37:25.138
(light music)

446
00:37:36.280 --> 00:37:39.500
Over the years, the woodpeckers
have pecked away thousands

447
00:37:39.500 --> 00:37:41.730
of little holes into the
trunk and filled them

448
00:37:41.730 --> 00:37:43.280
with acorns.

449
00:37:43.280 --> 00:37:45.570
The size and shape of
the openings are trimmed

450
00:37:45.570 --> 00:37:47.973
to the precise size of the acorns.

451
00:37:54.238 --> 00:37:57.245
But not every acorn fits into every hole.

452
00:37:57.245 --> 00:37:59.912
(bird knocking)

453
00:38:12.681 --> 00:38:15.014
The birds are being watched.

454
00:38:31.989 --> 00:38:34.580
For California ground
squirrels, a storage tree

455
00:38:34.580 --> 00:38:38.253
full of nuts like this is
a table set for dining.

456
00:38:48.970 --> 00:38:50.963
But it's not so easy.

457
00:38:52.515 --> 00:38:55.265
(dramatic music)

458
00:39:08.290 --> 00:39:11.143
The woodpeckers have stationed a guard.

459
00:39:14.640 --> 00:39:17.457
For now the intruder is kept off.

460
00:39:19.959 --> 00:39:23.317
But a squirrel doesn't
give up quite so easily.

461
00:39:27.090 --> 00:39:31.077
Wait for a moment until the
woodpecker is distracted and go.

462
00:39:34.956 --> 00:39:37.706
(dramatic music)

463
00:39:42.760 --> 00:39:44.770
The coast is still clear.

464
00:39:44.770 --> 00:39:46.883
It's time to get down to business.

465
00:39:48.010 --> 00:39:50.760
(dramatic music)

466
00:40:15.368 --> 00:40:17.393
Again, nothing.

467
00:40:19.030 --> 00:40:21.740
The woodpecker can quickly
get rid of the traces

468
00:40:21.740 --> 00:40:22.713
of the attack.

469
00:40:26.400 --> 00:40:30.733
Now it's time to wait and
look as innocent as possible.

470
00:40:37.120 --> 00:40:40.903
The next attempt starts in
the woodpecker's blind spot.

471
00:40:42.530 --> 00:40:44.663
The guard hasn't spotted the squirrel.

472
00:40:47.297 --> 00:40:50.297
(suspenseful music)

473
00:41:01.341 --> 00:41:02.841
No rash movements.

474
00:41:03.828 --> 00:41:06.828
(suspenseful music)

475
00:41:19.930 --> 00:41:22.320
The tactic pays off.

476
00:41:22.320 --> 00:41:25.903
Quickly fill your cheeks
before the woodpecker notices.

477
00:41:39.050 --> 00:41:41.287
Don't lose your cool now.

478
00:41:47.877 --> 00:41:52.294
(dramatic music and birds squawking)

479
00:42:01.009 --> 00:42:04.170
All you can do now is bear
the pain, close your eyes

480
00:42:04.170 --> 00:42:05.270
and hope for the best.

481
00:42:23.570 --> 00:42:24.413
Made it.

482
00:42:28.680 --> 00:42:32.113
Thick fur also protects
you from sharp beaks.

483
00:42:35.050 --> 00:42:38.050
A little cheeky, a little painful,

484
00:42:38.050 --> 00:42:39.713
but somehow you pulled through.

485
00:42:40.957 --> 00:42:43.457
(light music)

486
00:43:04.190 --> 00:43:05.580
The Alps.

487
00:43:05.580 --> 00:43:08.493
The meadows above the treeline
are the home of marmots.

488
00:43:09.490 --> 00:43:11.193
They too are squirrels.

489
00:43:17.550 --> 00:43:20.470
Alpine marmots spend the
summer days literally

490
00:43:20.470 --> 00:43:22.480
fattening themselves.

491
00:43:22.480 --> 00:43:26.600
The alpine pastures are
their land of milk and honey.

492
00:43:26.600 --> 00:43:29.720
Good provisions are
necessary, for here there will

493
00:43:29.720 --> 00:43:32.029
soon be meter-high snow.

494
00:43:32.029 --> 00:43:34.529
(light music)

495
00:43:42.520 --> 00:43:45.713
The most valuable tidbits
are defended vehemently.

496
00:43:46.980 --> 00:43:49.730
(dramatic music)

497
00:44:01.570 --> 00:44:04.370
Layers of fat are good for the winter.

498
00:44:04.370 --> 00:44:07.570
But it does mean you get
out of breath easily.

499
00:44:07.570 --> 00:44:09.690
Marmots don't like heat.

500
00:44:09.690 --> 00:44:13.690
Their comfort zone is
the cool mountain region.

501
00:44:13.690 --> 00:44:16.320
They have few sweat glands
and have a hard time

502
00:44:16.320 --> 00:44:18.303
getting rid of stored body heat.

503
00:44:25.440 --> 00:44:28.710
Marmots live close together so there are

504
00:44:28.710 --> 00:44:30.203
often skirmishes.

505
00:44:34.920 --> 00:44:38.310
Then the slow-paced alpine residents turn

506
00:44:38.310 --> 00:44:40.915
into bear-like sumo wrestlers.

507
00:44:40.915 --> 00:44:43.915
(suspenseful music)

508
00:44:55.230 --> 00:44:58.213
They bite, scuffle and shove.

509
00:44:59.280 --> 00:45:02.010
If one starts, a pack quickly forms

510
00:45:02.010 --> 00:45:05.150
and then everyone fights each other.

511
00:45:05.150 --> 00:45:07.160
The opponents try to knock each other over

512
00:45:07.160 --> 00:45:08.763
with targeted attacks.

513
00:45:13.300 --> 00:45:16.500
With marmots as with real sumo wrestlers,

514
00:45:16.500 --> 00:45:18.296
big is beautiful.

515
00:45:18.296 --> 00:45:21.046
(dramatic music)

516
00:45:34.574 --> 00:45:39.250
A heavier dead weight has
advantages when you're wrestling.

517
00:45:39.250 --> 00:45:42.293
Strength and agility
take care of the rest.

518
00:45:43.390 --> 00:45:45.150
Fit and fat.

519
00:45:45.150 --> 00:45:47.930
For marmots, these
attributes are not at odds

520
00:45:47.930 --> 00:45:48.763
with each other.

521
00:45:53.840 --> 00:45:56.836
The squirrels of the
world are as different

522
00:45:56.836 --> 00:45:59.360
as they can be.

523
00:45:59.360 --> 00:46:02.580
But they all have one thing in common,

524
00:46:02.580 --> 00:46:05.083
astonishing adaptability.

525
00:46:06.240 --> 00:46:08.800
Some of it is hereditary.

526
00:46:08.800 --> 00:46:13.310
But much of it is learned
or simply tried out.

527
00:46:13.310 --> 00:46:16.083
They learn about danger
and how to find things

528
00:46:16.083 --> 00:46:19.550
that are edible early
on from their mothers.

529
00:46:19.550 --> 00:46:23.763
But later too, they're
clever and quick to learn.

530
00:46:26.520 --> 00:46:30.100
When the time comes to
find new feeding grounds,

531
00:46:30.100 --> 00:46:31.523
they're second to none.

532
00:46:32.390 --> 00:46:35.320
They are true survival artists.

533
00:46:35.320 --> 00:46:38.653
Their shrewdness and
cleverness are legendary.

534
00:46:39.510 --> 00:46:43.000
Squirrels have become one of
the most successful animal

535
00:46:43.000 --> 00:46:47.373
families and live almost
all over the world today.

536
00:46:48.477 --> 00:46:51.060
(upbeat music)

537
00:47:04.600 --> 00:47:09.250
Our Eurasian squirrels have
also found new territories

538
00:47:09.250 --> 00:47:11.963
though they quickly reach their limits.

539
00:47:15.130 --> 00:47:20.130
Time to dare one last mighty
jump into the world of people,

540
00:47:25.770 --> 00:47:27.133
into our cities.

541
00:47:28.960 --> 00:47:31.770
The asphalt jungle doesn't
look like the optimal

542
00:47:31.770 --> 00:47:34.660
living space at first but squirrels are no

543
00:47:34.660 --> 00:47:36.660
big city neurotics.

544
00:47:36.660 --> 00:47:39.763
They take urban life
from the practical side.

545
00:47:46.760 --> 00:47:50.063
And certainly appreciate
being close to us humans.

546
00:48:02.590 --> 00:48:05.360
This is based on reciprocity.

547
00:48:05.360 --> 00:48:08.430
The little fellows are very
popular in the nature poor

548
00:48:08.430 --> 00:48:10.570
world of big cities.

549
00:48:10.570 --> 00:48:13.530
More and more people set up feeding sites

550
00:48:13.530 --> 00:48:16.043
to have the comical vagrants nearby.

551
00:48:26.145 --> 00:48:28.645
(light music)

552
00:48:35.480 --> 00:48:38.370
Today squirrels have gotten used to life

553
00:48:38.370 --> 00:48:39.203
in the city.

554
00:48:42.380 --> 00:48:45.140
They are even less
stressed than their fellows

555
00:48:45.140 --> 00:48:49.170
outside town despite the
continuous disruptions

556
00:48:49.170 --> 00:48:50.453
and street noise.

557
00:48:51.800 --> 00:48:54.200
The city offers them plenty of food

558
00:48:54.200 --> 00:48:58.293
because where there are
people, there is always food.

559
00:49:21.150 --> 00:49:25.460
And who knows, maybe their
adaptability will soon lead

560
00:49:25.460 --> 00:49:28.750
to the evolution of a new ultra relaxed

561
00:49:28.750 --> 00:49:29.863
big city squirrel.

562
00:49:31.070 --> 00:49:34.268
At any rate, they're
always full of surprises.

563
00:49:34.268 --> 00:49:36.768
(light music)
